Actually, the best approach: use `node:module` to check if the current file is the main module. In ESM:
```js
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
if (require.main === module) {
  app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(...));
}
```

Wait, `require.main === module` might not work reliably with ESM. Let me use a simpler approach:

```js
const isMain = process.argv[1] === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
if (isMain) {
  app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(...));
}
```

Actually, for `node --test`, `process.argv[1]` would be the test file path, not `server.js`. So this would prevent auto-starting. And for `node api/server.js`, `process.argv[1]` would be the server file path. This should work.

But wait — `node --test` also needs to import `server.js`. `process.argv[1]` when running `node --test verificacoes/atividades.spec.js` would be `verificacoes/atividades.spec.js` (or its absolute path), not `api/server.js`. So the auto-start would be skipped. Good.

Let me implement this approach.

