content.text:
Wait, but when the test calls `reset()`, it resets the clock to `2026-10-13T09:00:00-03:00`. Then the test creates an activity with encontro at `2026-10-19T19:00:00-03:00`. This is valid (within event period). Then it advances the clock to `2026-10-19T19:30:00-03:00`. At this time, `calcularSituacao` should return `em_andamento` because `agora (19:30) >= primeiroInicio (19:00) && agora (19:30) < ultimoFim (20:00)`.

But wait — the `reset()` function calls `POST /_teste/reset`. Looking at the reset handler in server.js:
```js
app.post('/_teste/reset', (req, res) => {
  relogio = { agora: '2026-10-13T09:00:00-03:00' };
  atividades = [];
  proximoId = 1;
  res.status(204).send();
});
```

This resets `relogio` to `2026-10-13T09:00:00-03:00`. Then when the test advances the clock to `2026-10-19T19:30:00-03:00`, it should work.

But `calcularSituacao` uses `relogio.agora` which is set by `PUT /_teste/relogio`. The `PUT` handler updates `relogio = { agora }`. So after the PUT, `relogio.agora` should be `2026-10-19T19:30:00-03:00`.

Let me run the tests again.

